<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Dayuan</h1><p class="st-meta">200 BCE – 300 CE (approx.) · capital Ershi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The ancient kingdom of the Ferghana valley, famed in China for its 'heavenly horses', which provoked the Han dynasty's longest-range war.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Dayuan, the Chinese name for the farming people of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-ferghana">Ferghana</a> valley, was described by the envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian">Zhang Qian</a> around 128 BCE as a land of over 70 walled towns and several hundred thousand people. Its population may have included descendants of Greek settlers from Alexander's era, though this is debated.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The people grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, wheat and grapes, made wine and bred tall, fast horses that the Han court prized as 'blood-sweating' heavenly horses. A king ruled with nobles from the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ershi">Ershi</a>. In 104 BCE Dayuan refused to supply horses, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> sent the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-guangli">Li Guangli</a>, who after two campaigns besieged Ershi in 102 BCE, forcing the nobles to kill their king Wugua and hand over horses.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Dayuan remained a tributary of the Han for a time and appears in Chinese records into the 3rd century CE. Thereafter <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-ferghana">Ferghana</a> was ruled by local dynasties that became the later Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Ferghana</a>, still noted for its horses.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 200 BCE – 300 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central Asia and the Steppe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:55.72%;width:9.95%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>200 BCE – 300 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ershi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Wugua</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Eastern Iranian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, <a href="/countries/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a>, <a href="/countries/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dayuan, Ta-yuan, Ferghana (ancient)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=50&amp;focus=kingdom-of-dayuan" aria-label="Kingdom of Dayuan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6235 696 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Dayuan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7769 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6555 952h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6555 952h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Uzbekistan. <a href="/atlas?year=50&amp;focus=kingdom-of-dayuan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

